// Per-crop nutrition bridge (v0.663) -- closes gap #3 in docs/design/homestead-solo-design.md
// section 7. One entry per FOOD crop id in data/plants.csv (vegetables, fruits, grains,
// legumes, edible herbs, and the three edible mushrooms). Skips alien / decorative / fiber /
// tree-only / medicinal-remedy types -- those are not dietary food. This is the data
// self-sufficiency.md's "What data we'd add" section calls for: a per-crop calorie/yield->kcal
// bridge so the food loop can be COMPUTED from crop data instead of trusting the hand-typed
// "+120 kcal/d" strings in the machine catalog (data/machines/home.ron). Loaded by
// src/systems/self_sufficiency.rs (feature-neutral ron+serde). Data-driven (infinite-of-X):
// add a crop row here, no code change. This file says what 100 g of a harvest holds; how many
// grams a plot harvests is NOT here: see FIELDS below.
//
// TRAPS FOUND 2026-09-15 BY A WRITER DOING ARITHMETIC OFF THIS FILE. Read these
// before you multiply anything out.
//
//   1. RESOLVED 2026-09-26: the "yield unit" is gone. This file carried a
//      `grams_per_yield_unit` that meant a discrete object for a vegetable and
//      a sown patch for a grain (wheat at 8 to 20 units of 50 g made one plant
//      400 to 1,000 g of grain), and it disagreed with items.csv, whose wheat
//      item weighs 500 g. plants.csv yield_min/yield_max are now a cited harvest
//      PER PLANT, counted in items of the crop's harvest item, and a bed, tray or
//      field plot holds as many plants as fit at plants.csv area_per_plant_m2
//      (sources in data/garden/yields.ron). One weight for a harvest everywhere.
//
//   2. THE GENERIC `bean` ROW IS NOT TRACEABLE TO ONE USDA ROW and is not
//      flagged as an estimate. Its calories and carbohydrate match USDA kidney
//      beans exactly, its protein matches USDA black beans exactly, and its fat
//      matches neither. `kidney_bean` and `lentil` also drift from their USDA
//      rows. The specific rows are the ones to trust.
//
//   3. WHAT THIS FILE CANNOT EXPRESS, which matters for what the simulation can
//      ever tell a player: not one micronutrient, so the game can say whether
//      you grew enough calories and never whether you grew enough vitamin C,
//      which in real life is the one that kills people. Also no amino acid
//      profile (so the grain-and-legume complement cannot be modelled), no
//      cooked/raw state or retention factors (boiled greens retain 95 to 100
//      percent of folate if the water is used and about 30 percent if it is
//      drained, and that is unrepresentable), no refuse fraction, no fibre and
//      no moisture content.
//
//   4. type=tree CROPS ARE EXCLUDED and some of them are food. oak, maple and
//      palm are skipped as trees, while their own plants.csv descriptions name
//      acorns, sap and fruit, and acorns are taught as food in five Library
//      guides.
//
// FIELDS (all per 100 g EDIBLE PORTION, raw unless a note says otherwise):
//   plant_id             - MUST match a row id in data/plants.csv (a typo = a dead entry;
//                          enforced by a unit test that cross-checks every id against the CSV).
//   calories_per_100g    - kcal per 100 g. USDA FoodData Central magnitude.
//   protein_g/fat_g/carbs_g - macros per 100 g.
//
// WHAT A HARVEST WEIGHS (2026-09-26): plants.csv yield_min/yield_max (items per plant) x the
// harvest item's items.csv weight_kg x the plants in the plot. food_supply_kcal_per_day() in
// src/systems/self_sufficiency.rs reads exactly that (farming::units::plot_harvest_kg), the same
// figure the harvest hands the player and the nutrient model bills the soil for.
//
// POTATO CHECK (test self_sufficiency::tests::potato_bed_calories_come_from_the_cited_yield): a
// 2 m2 potato bed holds 8 plants at UMN's spacing and harvests about 7.2 kg every 90 days (NASS
// North Dakota yields), 61.5 kcal a day cropped back to back. home.ron's potato_grow_bed claims
// "+120 kcal/d": about twice what the cited yield gives. The old calibration here reached 0.96x
// of that claim by modelling a bed as "~1.0 harvested tuber-unit/day"; the claim, not the yield,
// is what is off.
//
// FLAGGED estimates (not cleanly grounded in a single USDA food row -- see the session report):
//   * Beverage/flavor crops consumed in tiny amounts or brewed: coffee, cacao, tea, hop,
//     chamomile, vanilla, saffron. Their per-100g numbers describe the consumed dry product;
//     dietary contribution is near-zero. hop is a brewing crop (not eaten) -> nominal 0 kcal.
//   * Dried-herb rows (oregano, sage) use dried-spice magnitude (USDA lists these dried).
//   * lavender has no standard USDA food row -> estimated from comparable edible flowers.
//   * corn uses SWEET-corn (fresh) values; field/dent maize as a dry grain would be ~365 kcal.
//   * bean/lentil/chickpea/soybean use DRY mature-seed values (protein-crop role).
//   * NO USDA FOOD ROW EXISTS for these -- values come from the published composition
//     literature named per line and are the least certain numbers in this file:
//     tepary_bean, haskap, sea_buckthorn, oca, sugar_beet, finger_millet, rapeseed,
//     camelina, bambara_groundnut.
//   * horseradish uses USDA "Horseradish, prepared". It is a condiment eaten in gram
//     quantities, so its per-unit kcal overstates real dietary contribution the same way
//     the dried-herb rows do.
//   * runner_bean uses GREEN SNAP-POD values (that is how it is eaten and how its
//     plants.csv row is written), unlike the six dry bean rows beside it.
//   * hazelnut and walnut use KERNEL nutrition while their items.csv harvest items are
//     weighed IN THE SHELL, so until the item or this row changes basis the food model
//     counts the shell's weight as kernel (about 2x). Their grams_per_yield_unit used to
//     hold the kernel fraction; that column is gone.
//   * plantain uses USDA "Plantains, GREEN, raw" (152 kcal) not the ripe/general row
//     (122 kcal), because the crop is harvested green and cooked as a starch. This is the
//     banana calibration anchor: 152 vs banana 89 is the real starch difference.
//   * triticale (USDA 336/13.1/1.8/72.1) lands between wheat (340/13.7) and rye (338/10.3)
//     exactly as a wheat-rye hybrid should -- that agreement is the grain anchor check.
//
// COVERAGE: every non-alien / non-decorative / non-fiber / non-tree / non-medicinal row in
// data/plants.csv now has an entry here (142 of 189 plant rows). The excluded types are not
// dietary food. Adding a food crop to plants.csv without adding it here silently scores it
// at 0 kcal, which is why the coverage check matters more than any single number below.
